import{_ as s,A as d,o as n,c as r,X as a,C as o}from"./chunks/framework.pSp8ynCZ.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Data and execution model","titleTemplate":":title · CellPack Rust","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"rust/docs/concepts.md","filePath":"concepts.md"}'),c={name:"rust/docs/concepts.md"};function i(l,e,p,u,h,m){const t=d("DocDiagram");return n(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a('<h1 id="data-and-execution-model" tabindex="-1">Data and execution model <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-and-execution-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Data and execution model&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="matrices-chunks-records-and-pages" tabindex="-1">Matrices, chunks, records, and pages <a class="header-anchor" href="#matrices-chunks-records-and-pages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Matrices, chunks, records, and pages&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>.cpk</code> file holds one matrix. Rows are divided into chunks, each sharing a set of coding models and an optional column permutation. Each row has an independently decodable record; empty rows may have no record bytes. Records are packed onto pages so a reader needs only the pages covering its record and the chunk&#39;s metadata.</p><p>Independent decoding removes dependencies between records. It still requires the matching chunk metadata and decode tables. Neighboring records may share a page.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.cpk</code></td><td>One matrix stream, finalized by <code>finish</code></td></tr><tr><td>Chunk</td><td>A group of rows with common coding models</td></tr><tr><td>Record</td><td>One row&#39;s compressed representation</td></tr><tr><td>Page</td><td>The unit used for aligned reads and cache frames</td></tr><tr><td>Cached row</td><td>A decoded input row in its stored dtype</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="four-column-spaces" tabindex="-1">Four column spaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#four-column-spaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Four column spaces&quot;">​</a></h2>',6)),o(t,{name:"column-spaces"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<p>Storage reads normally restore source order. A loader takes a source-to-standard map for each matrix and an output-to-standard list for each schema. It composes these maps when constructed. Name lookup and vocabulary construction remain with the application.</p><h2 id="cells-positions-accesses-and-batches" tabindex="-1">Cells, positions, accesses, and batches <a class="header-anchor" href="#cells-positions-accesses-and-batches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cells, positions, accesses, and batches&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>CellId</code> is a global <code>u32</code> row id assigned by a data source. A position is a logical place in the requested sequence and can refer to a cell more than once. With <code>S</code> output schemas, every position has <code>S</code> accesses:</p>',3)),o(t,{name:"access-order"}),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<p>An output slot collects every schema&#39;s output for up to <code>slot_cells</code> consecutive positions. A batch is a lease on a completed slot. The last batch may have fewer rows. The schemas of a position may request the same cell or different cells.</p><h2 id="bounded-output-and-planned-reuse" tabindex="-1">Bounded output and planned reuse <a class="header-anchor" href="#bounded-output-and-planned-reuse" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bounded output and planned reuse&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The output ring has a fixed number of slots. A producer cannot overwrite a slot while a consumer holds its lease. Slow consumption applies backpressure.</p><p>A cache plan assigns input pages and decoded input rows to frames over intervals of accesses. Workers execute those reads, waits, and decodes. <code>Planning::None</code> disables planned caching but still uses worker-private I/O buffers and the output ring.</p><p>The output ring, the planned cache, and the operating system&#39;s page cache serve different purposes. See <a href="/cellpack-docs/rust/docs/performance.html">memory and performance</a> for their budgets.</p>',5))])}const b=s(c,[["render",i]]);export{f as __pageData,b as default};
